Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// This strategy tracks the full generated answer in `generated`, whether we are
// currently inside an arithmetic-computation span in `insideConstrainedOut`, and
// the delimiter-free contents of that active span in `currentConstrainedOut`.
// Outside a span, it generates mostly freely but proactively opens `<<` when the
// open-delimiter logit is close to the current argmax or when the sampled token
// itself contains `<<`; this biases the model toward writing explicit step-by-
// step computations. Inside a span, it first checks for parser completion and
// closes with `>>` only in that case; otherwise it tries the current argmax when
// parser-valid and falls back to `ConstrainedStep` when needed. The tracked
// constrained suffix lets us prove parser validity and that the active span
// matches the suffix of the emitted text.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the unconstrained branch, if we open a span proactively or
//   after sampling `<<`, we set currentConstrainedOut := [], which is valid by
//   the method precondition parser.IsValidPrefix([]); otherwise we remain
//   outside, so the implication is vacuous. In the complete-prefix branch,
//   CloseConstrainedSpan resets insideConstrainedOut to false, again making the
//   implication vacuous. In the speculative constrained branch, we call
//   AppendConstrainedToken only after IsTokenValidNext says the argmax is valid;
//   in the fallback branch, ConstrainedStep provides a parser-valid next token
//   (or EOS, which breaks), so appending preserves validity.
// suffix: In every opening branch we append only "<<" and set
//   currentConstrainedOut := [], so the length-0 suffix of generated matches the
//   current constrained contents. In the close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan
//   appends ">>" and resets currentConstrainedOut to [], so the implication is
//   vacuous afterward. In both constrained append branches,
//   AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to generated and to
//   currentConstrainedOut, preserving the suffix equality.
// cost: In the unconstrained raw-logit branch we use GenerateLogits and logit
//   queries, which do not bump helpers.cost, then sample with
//   ChooseNextTokenUnconstrained and manually do helpers.cost := helpers.cost + 1;
//   we also increment steps once, so helpers.cost <= steps is preserved. In the
//   close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan bumps helpers.cost by 1 internally and we
//   increment steps once. In the speculative constrained-argmax branch we again
//   manually bump helpers.cost after ChooseNextTokenUnconstrained and increment
//   steps once; AppendConstrainedToken itself does not bump cost. In the
//   fallback constrained branch, ConstrainedStep bumps by 1 and steps also grows
//   by 1.
// progress: In the unconstrained branch we append at most one token ("<<" or the
//   sampled token) and then increment steps by 1. In the close branch we append
//   exactly one token ">>" and increment steps by 1. In each constrained branch
//   we append at most one token before incrementing steps by 1; if EOS is
//   returned we break without appending, which also preserves the bound.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps := 0;

while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> generated[|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|..] == currentConstrainedOut
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  invariant cost == 0
  invariant helpers.cost <= steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
